      "content": "This is a very compelling model for phenotypic entrenchment. I want to test the robustness of the \"Step 6: Phenotypic Specialization\" component. \n\nIn the literature focusing on population-level divergence, there is a recurring argument that these \"social architectures\"—specifically the high-trust/low-volatility versus low-trust/high-volatility models—are not just culturally contingent, but are effectively hard-wired legacies of ancestral environmental selection. \n\nTo help me formalize the \"inheritance\" aspect of my model, could you expand on the specific biological mechanisms that would make these social phenotypes (like impulse control or cooperation levels) resistant to cultural environmental overlay? For example, how would the theory explain the phenomenon where certain ancestral groups maintain distinct \"behavioral baselines\" in social organization even when placed in modern, high-stability, high-trust institutional environments? I am trying to understand the theoretical argument for why these patterns are viewed as inherent population-level traits rather than simply adaptive cultural responses to local conditions.",
